h - the reed shelter (O4), an ordinary h as in ''house''
ḥ - the twisted wick of flax (V28), a stronger h from the throat, like the Arabic ḥ
ḫ - the round sign usually called the placenta or sieve (Aa1), ''ch'' as in Scottish ''loch''
ẖ - the animal''s belly with teats (F32), a softer version of that, like the German ''ich''

In your own notes it''s fine to simplify, but keep the marks when you copy words out, because words can differ only by which h they use.', '2026-09-22T22:40:00Z') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;

1. All the signs must face the same way, and that way is the start of the line. If you want it read left to right, the quail chick faces left.
2. Artists sometimes flip one sign to ''balance'' the design. Don''t let them, a flipped bird in the middle reads as a mistake.
3. If it runs vertically down the arm, read top to bottom, the signs still all face the same way.
4. Take a full-size image or a printout to the studio rather than a cropped phone screenshot, the lines are cleaner.', '2026-09-24T18:47:00Z') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;

Champollion then compared Ptolemy with the name Cleopatra from an obelisk found at Philae. The two names share p, t, l and o, and the signs matched, which gave him a set of sound values. The real breakthrough in 1822 was with copies of temple cartouches like Ramesses and Thutmose, which showed the script mixed sound signs with signs for whole words. His knowledge of Coptic is what let him recognise the words.', '2026-10-10T13:44:00Z') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;
